      "s": "Bloomberg Tech",
      "t": "SpaceX Starship Lifts Off for Key Orbital Test",
      "d": "Elon Musk's SpaceX launched its massive Starship rocket Monday morning on its first orbital flight test. This rocket is carrying 26 satellites that are to be deployed into orbit. This is the launch from the Starbase facility in South Texas. (Source: Bloomberg)",
      "u": "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/videos/2026-09-28/spacex-starship-lifts-off-for-key-orbital-test-video",
      "m": "09. 28. PM 01:39",
      "ts": 1790602750000,
      "fullText": ""
    },
    {
      "s": "TechCrunch",
      "t": "SpaceX&#8217;s Starship rocket reaches orbit for the first time",
      "d": "Though not without some drama along the way.",
      "u": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/28/spacexs-starship-rocket-reaches-orbit-for-the-first-time/",
      "m": "09. 28. PM 01:19",
      "ts": 1790601558000,
      "fullText": "SpaceX&#8217;s Starship rocket reached Earth orbit for the first time, though not without some drama along the way. The company&#8217;s mega-rocket launched into space from South Texas early Monday morning and the take off ticked all the early checkboxes for a successful launch. But shortly after the upper stage of the spacecraft separated, it lost one of its six Raptor engines. While Starship could still reach orbit without one engine, the company initially called off the attempt, only to decide to keep pushing just a few minutes later. The rocket ultimately reached orbit and successfully deployed 26 new Starlink satellites. Roughly 1.5 hours into the flight, SpaceX decided to bring the upper stage back to Earth early, instead of letting it orbit for around nine hours as planned. &#8220;Flight teams will deorbit Starship safely to our pre-cleared area of the Pacific Ocean after 3 hours in orbit,&#8221; the company wrote on X. Reaching orbit is a critical milestone that could let SpaceX start using Starship for regular commercial missions, and, importantly, inch closer to its goal of making the mega-rocket fully reusable. SpaceX CEO Elon Musk is orienting the entire company around Starship, which has become central to the company&#8217;s plans to operate moon missions for NASA, and far-out ideas like putting data center satellites in orbit. Prior to the launch, SpaceX had planned to have the Starship upper stage orbit the Earth six times over nine hours while deploying 26 third-generation Starlinks into the satellite company&#8217;s constellation of internet satellites. The Ship stage dispensed all of those V3 Starlink satellites during the Monday flight and was able to connect to all 26. They are supposed to start servicing Starlink customers in the next few weeks. These upgraded Starlink satellites are more powerful than the prior versions SpaceX has used to build its 10,000-strong constellation. The company has said that one full Starship launch of V3 satellites "
    },
